<h2>What the SERP preview shows</h2><p>This preview gives you a fast visual check of the three elements users usually notice first: the page URL, title and description. Search engines can rewrite titles and descriptions, so treat the preview as an editing aid rather than a guaranteed rendering.</p><h2>How to use this tool</h2><ol><li>Enter the title you plan to publish.</li><li>Add the canonical page URL.</li><li>Write a concise description that accurately summarizes the page.</li><li>Review the preview and character indicators, then edit for clarity rather than chasing an exact count.</li></ol><h2>Write for meaning before character count</h2><p>Pixel widths vary by letters, device and search layout. Character ranges are therefore useful guardrails, not strict ranking rules. Put the primary topic naturally near the beginning, avoid repetitive wording, and make the result useful to a person deciding whether to click.</p><h2>Why snippets can differ in Google</h2><p>A search engine may choose other on-page text when it believes that text better answers a specific query. A strong title and description still matter because they give search systems clear metadata and give your team a controlled default.</p>
